leather book alchemy~
a book of alchemy~
A leather-bound book has been left nearby.~
~
14 0 a 0
0 0 0 0 0
3 3 650 100 0 1
E
two~
First Circle:
-------------
"&gRejuvenation&w" - Sulfurous ash, nightshade, blood moss and mandrake root
"&gShield of Good&w" - Sulfurous ash, nightshade, garlic and ginseng
"&gShield of Evil&w" - Sulfurous ash, black pearl, garlic and ginseng
"&gTraveller's Focus&w" - Black pearl, blood moss, mandrake root and spider's silk

Second Circle:
--------------
"&gFighter's Bane&w" - Sulfurous ash, nightshade and garlic
"&gHaven&w" - Sulfurous ash, garlic and ginseng
"&gWeariness&w" - Nightshade, black pearl and spider's silk
"&gEthereal Cloak&w" - Nightshade, blood moss and mandrake root
"&gFighter's Remedy&w" - Garlic, ginseng and mandrake root

Third Circle:
-------------
"&gEthereal Sight&w" - Sulfurous ash and nightshade
"&gSelf Restoration&w" - Ginseng and spider's silk
~
E
one~
&gBlack pearl&w - Born of an oyster bathed in water magic, a black pearl channels
energy of projection in manners both foreceful and absurd.

&gBlood moss&w - The energy of blood rolled in battle imbues a common type of moss
with a magical potential that is suitable for spells of movement.

&gGarlic&w - For eons, common garlic has warded off creatures of undead
enchantment, though some have developed resistances over time.

&gGinseng&w - In ancient eras, ginseng was crushed into a fine powder and added to
the ale of warriors on the eve of battle.

&gMandrake root&w - Known to mages of yore as the Root of Life for its aptitude in
binding spells of power.

&gNightshade&w - A deadly plant if eaten, nightshade can bind illusions or, if
used by different method, spells of poison.

&gSpider's silk&w - Little in the natural world has the tensile strength of a
spider's webbing.

&gSulfurous ash&w - Sulfurous ash holds the energy of friction from the volcanic
eruption that begot it.
~
E
leather book alchemy~
The first few pages of the book give a brief history of alchemy, most of which
does not interest you. There are, however, bits and pieces of information on
pages one and two which you might find useful.

Page one deals primarily with reagents, whose unique properties form magical
elixirs when bound together in the correct combination.

Page two lists detailed ingredients for a number of the more common potions
discovered over time. The text goes on to explain that not all combinations
have been tested and it is rumored that several magical elixirs still await
discovery.
~
